<h3>The Question</h3>
<p>
    Backlog <a href="/backlog/3456-cap-concurrent-heavy-commands-across-dispatched-lanes-a-capa/">#3456</a>
    asks how to cap the number of genuinely CPU/IO-heavy commands — <code>check:standards</code>,
    <code>verify-lane</code>/<code>test:unit</code>, <code>npm ci</code>/<code>npm install</code>, a Playwright
    visual-capture pass — that may run <em>at the same instant</em> across independently-dispatched lane
    processes, as the mechanical dispatcher scales up parallel lane usage. Lane availability and CPU/heavy-
    command capacity are two different resources; today only the first is throttled. Before naming a default
    for each of the item's three ratifiable forks (plus two adjacent calls settled as "supported by
    default," not ratified), this survey grounds the design in shipped admission-control prior art —
    job schedulers, resource pools, and this repo's own existing cross-process locks.
</p>

<h3>Recommendation (feeding #3456's forks)</h3>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Fork 1 — an explicit, named list</strong> (not yet per-command-weighted — see Finding 2's
        note below), mirroring Bazel's declared-cost resource pool rather than a uniform per-job slot or a
        not-yet-buildable adaptive classifier.</li>
    <li><strong>Fork 2 — gate at heavy-command-invocation time</strong>, mirroring GNU Make's jobserver: a
        token is acquired immediately before a job runs, not at process start, keeping lane leasing and CPU
        capacity genuinely decoupled.</li>
    <li><strong>Fork 3 — a new, distinct, immediately-surfaced signal</strong>, mirroring GitHub Actions'
        first-class queued/pending job state, not silence.</li>
</ol>
<p>
    Two further calls are <strong>not</strong> numbered forks — they read like a fork at first, but the
    item's own two-confusion screen dissolved each into "what v1 ships + real, separately-prioritized
    future work," so this survey backs them as "Supported by default" grounding, not as a competing
    recommendation a ratifier picks between:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Heavy-command classification stays the explicit list (Fork 1) for v1</strong> — a
        measured/adaptive classifier (GNU Parallel's <code>--load</code>) is real future work, not built
        only because the measurement layer doesn't exist here yet.</li>
    <li><strong>The admission cap is a fixed, machine-overridable number</strong>, mirroring Bazel's
        measured-once host-fact default; real adaptive load-sampling needs a dedicated instantaneous-signal
        layer this repo doesn't have yet — the same readiness gap as the classification point above, not a
        merit call.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Finding 1 — GNU Make's jobserver: a cross-process counting semaphore, bound at job-start, not process-start</h3>
<p>
    GNU Make's <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/make/manual/html_node/Job-Slots.html">jobserver
    protocol</a> coordinates parallelism across recursive <code>make</code> invocations with no daemon: a pipe
    is pre-loaded with one single-character token per available job slot, propagated to child processes via the
    <code>MAKEFLAGS</code> environment variable (<code>--jobserver-auth=</code>). A client acquires a token —
    reads one character from the pipe — immediately before starting a job, and releases it — writes one
    character back — immediately after the job finishes. Crucially, every invoked <code>make</code> process
    always has one implicit job slot reserved before it starts, so a caller can always make forward progress
    without even contacting the jobserver.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Implication (Fork 2):</strong> the token exchange happens at <em>job</em>-start, not at
    <em>process</em>-start — exactly the operator's own framing that lane availability and CPU capacity are
    separate resources. A lane may always be acquired freely; only the heavy command itself should queue on the
    capacity semaphore, right before it runs.
</p>

<h3>Finding 2 — Bazel: a declared-cost resource pool, not a blanket job-slot count</h3>
<p>
    Bazel's <code>--local_cpu_resources</code>/<code>--local_ram_resources</code>
    (<a href="https://bazel.build/docs/user-manual">user manual</a>;
    <a href="https://jmmv.dev/2019/12/bazel-local-resources.html">how Bazel tracks local resources</a>) define a
    fixed local pool that defaults to a value measured once from the host (e.g. <code>HOST_CPUS-1</code>), not
    continuously re-sampled at runtime. Each action <em>declares its own cost</em> (<code>cpu=2</code>,
    <code>memory=HOST_RAM*.5</code>), and Bazel subtracts the cost from the tracker when a spawn starts and
    returns it when the spawn finishes.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Implication (Fork 1, and the "supported by default" cap-value call):</strong> a named list is
    the shipped, load-bearing pattern for Fork 1(a) — not a uniform cap applied to every command regardless
    of cost (Fork 1(b)). (Fork 1's own text starts this list as an equal-cost <em>set</em>, not yet
    per-command-weighted; declared weighting is real future refinement, mirroring the Bazel per-action-cost
    shape but not yet built here.) And the admission cap itself is a fixed, host-measured-once number with a
    configurable override — not something Bazel continuously re-samples against live load; this is the
    "supported by default" cap-value grounding, not a ratifiable fork branch.
</p>

<h3>Finding 3 — GNU Parallel's <code>--load</code>: real adaptive throttling needs its own dedicated signal, not raw load average</h3>
<p>
    GNU Parallel's <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/parallel/parallel_design.html">design docs</a> are
    explicit that its <code>--load</code> flag does <strong>not</strong> use the OS load average — load average
    rises too slowly to give a useful, instant read. Instead it polls <code>ps</code> for the count of threads
    in a running or blocked state (<code>R</code>/<code>D</code>/<code>O</code>), and busy-waits with an
    exponential backoff (capped at one second) between polls to avoid the poll itself burning CPU.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Implication (the "supported by default" cap-value call):</strong> real, shipped adaptive
    throttling is not "swap a constant for <code>os.loadavg()</code>" — it is a dedicated, nontrivial
    measurement-and-hysteresis layer that this repo does not have today (the same gap that excludes the
    adaptive heavy-command-classification call adjacent to Fork 1). That is evidence adaptive load-sampling
    is a real, deferred upgrade, not a reason to reject it in principle — and not a ratifiable fork branch
    here.
</p>

<h3>Finding 4 — GitHub Actions concurrency groups: a queued job is a first-class visible state</h3>
<p>
    GitHub Actions' <a href="https://docs.github.com/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-what-your-workflow-does/control-the-concurrency-of-workflows-and-jobs">concurrency
    groups</a> hold a job in a distinct, queryable <code>queued</code>/<code>pending</code> state rather than
    leaving it silent while it waits for a group slot, and a
    <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2026-05-07-github-actions-concurrency-groups-now-allow-larger-queues/">2026-05-07
    change</a> made that queue's depth itself configurable (up to 100 pending runs).
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Implication (Fork 3):</strong> the mainstream shape for "waiting on a concurrency gate" is a
    dedicated, visible signal — not process-level-only observation a supervisor has to go find by hand.
</p>

<h3>Finding 5 — this repo already ships two single-holder cross-process locks; the eventual mechanism is their natural N=cap generalization</h3>
<p>
    Two independently-designed, already-shipped mechanisms establish the primitive shape a later admission-queue
    build would generalize: <code>we:scripts/readiness/file-locks.mjs</code> (#1936, itself surveyed in
    <a href="/research/agent-file-lock-coordination/">Agent File-Lock Coordination</a>) reserves a path via an
    atomic lock directory, a heartbeat-TTL lease, and a same-machine PID-liveness fast-reclaim path layered on
    top (PID reuse means liveness alone is never the primary check). <code>we:scripts/conveyor/infra-blocked.mjs</code>'s
    <code>withInfraLock</code> takes an exclusive-create lock file with a stale-lock steal window and — its
    sharpest design principle — a hard timeout that <strong>proceeds anyway</strong> rather than risk
    deadlocking a tick.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Implication:</strong> both are <code>N=1</code> mutexes; nothing in this repo today generalizes one
    to an <code>N=cap</code> counting semaphore. That confirms this survey's own confirmed-gap grep (#3456's own
    text) at the mechanism level, and gives whichever fork's follow-on build item picks a mechanism a concrete
    starting shape — cited here as grounding, never as this decision's own scope (the exact mechanism stays
    explicitly out of #3456's ruling).
</p>

